        "# Evaluation and Coordination Benchmarks Notebook\n\n> Hands-on Build It and Exercises."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## Build It"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "`code/main.py` is a non-interactive walk-through:"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "- Simulates 3 multi-agent systems on a toy task."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "- Computes MARBLE-style milestone metrics for each."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "- Runs a contamination check by withholding tasks from a \"training\" set."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "- Compares to a random baseline explicitly."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "- Prints a benchmark-claims scorecard."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Run:"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```bash"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "python3 code/main.py"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Expected output: system scorecard with raw accuracy, milestone achievement, cost-per-task, vs-random baseline delta, and a contamination-check note."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## Exercises"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "1. Run `code/main.py`. Identify which of the three simulated systems has the best cost-per-milestone. Does it match the highest raw-accuracy system?\n2. Read MultiAgentBench (arXiv:2503.01935). For your own task domain, decide which of the four topologies MARBLE would recommend. Justify from the paper's results.\n3. Read the SWE-bench Pro paper. What specifically makes it contamination-resistant? Could the same technique be applied to other benchmarks you care about?\n4. Read COMMA's finding on multimodal coordination. Design a simple multimodal coordination task you could add to your internal benchmark. What would count as a useful signal?\n5. Apply the benchmark-claims checklist to one recent multi-agent paper's headline result. What grade would you give the claim?"
      ]
    }
